bug_report:
No diagnostic issued if the decl-specifier in the decl-specifier-seq of a for-range-declaration is register, static,or thread_local
The C++ standard requires the decl-specifier in the decl-specifier-seq of a for-range-declaration shall be either a type-specifier or constexpr ([stmt.ranged]/2).  GCC does so for most decl-specifiers (eg. "friend", "mutable", "extern", etc) but not for "register," "static," or "thread_local."  This is in C++14 mode (so, um, "register" isn't even a valid decl-specifier any more).

clang, MSVC, ICC all give appropriate diagnostics in this case. GCC (all versions tested, up to 8.0.1 20180117) remains mute.

Here's a sample bit of code that should not compile silently.

    void f() {
        int a[] = { 1, 2, 3 };
        for (register int& i : a) {
           // ...
        }
    }
